@* The mark for a destination, in one place.

   WHY A COMPONENT AND NOT INLINE SVG
   ==================================================================================
   The same five destinations appear in two navs -- the tab bar on a phone, the row of buttons on a
   desktop -- and both are supposed to be the same nav at two widths. NavMenu's own note commits to
   that: "a nav whose contents reshuffle between a laptop and a phone is one you have to re-learn."
   The contents already matched. The marks did not, because they were inline in the tab bar and
   absent from the desktop row.

   Copying the path data across would be the mistake PackMark was written to end: "path data copied
   into two files is path data that disagrees with itself after the first tweak." So the glyphs live
   here and both navs read from them, and the booster pack keeps coming from PackMark because it is
   in the wordmark too.

   currentColor throughout, no literal colours: the same drawing has to sit in body ink on a pale
   bar, white on a filled active button, and the accent on a selected tab, without any of the three
   saying anything about colour.

   A 24 viewBox and a 1.8-1.9 stroke on every mark, so they carry the same weight as each other at
   the same rendered size. The caller sets that size: 22px beside a tab label, 15px beside a nav
   button's word, where the mark is a hint at the word rather than the thing you aim at. *@

<svg class="@Class" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="@Size" height="@Size"
     focusable="false" aria-hidden="true">
    @switch (For)
    {
        @* Four panes: the grid the page opens as, which is the only one of these a reader has
           already seen full-size before they see the mark. *@
        case "collection":
            <rect x="3" y="3" width="7.5" height="7.5" rx="1.5" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" />
            <rect x="13.5" y="3" width="7.5" height="7.5" rx="1.5" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" />
            <rect x="3" y="13.5" width="7.5" height="7.5" rx="1.5" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" />
            <rect x="13.5" y="13.5" width="7.5" height="7.5" rx="1.5" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" />
            break;

        @* Four bars at four heights: one panel per set, each at its own standing, so the shape
           says "several things, each part-done". Deliberately not a percentage ring, which says
           one figure -- and one figure is what that page refuses to give, because the sets do not
           total. *@
        case "progress":
            <path d="M4 20V10M9.5 20V5M15 20v-8M20.5 20v-5"
                  fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.9" stroke-linecap="round" />
            break;

        @* A box seen in three-quarter view: a pack of five, which is what the page ranks. *@
        case "packs":
            <path d="M4 8.5 12 4l8 4.5v7L12 20l-8-4.5z" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linejoin="round" />
            <path d="M4 8.5 12 13l8-4.5M12 13v7" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linejoin="round" />
            break;

        case "more":
            <path d="M4 7h16M4 12h16M4 17h16" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.9" stroke-linecap="round" />
            break;

        @* ---- the More menu's nine -------------------------------------------------------
           Each of these was drawn, rendered at 15px beside the marks already in use, and kept
           or thrown away on what it looked like at that size rather than on the idea. The ones
           thrown away are named here, because the reason each was rejected is the reason the
           replacement looks the way it does -- and because the obvious drawing is usually the
           rejected one. *@

        @* A bookmark, not a heart: these are the cards you are hunting, not the ones you love.
           The heart is the grid's Want it mark and means something else. *@
        case "chase":
            <path d="M7 4h10a1 1 0 0 1 1 1v15l-6-4-6 4V5a1 1 0 0 1 1-1z"
                  fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linejoin="round" />
            break;

        @* One card in front of another: a deck is recognised by its face card, and the second
           edge is what says there is a stack behind it. *@
        case "decks":
            <g fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-linecap="round">
                <rect x="3.5" y="7" width="11.5" height="14" rx="1.6" />
                <path d="M7.5 4.5h10a1.6 1.6 0 0 1 1.6 1.6V17" />
            </g>
            break;

        @* A face-down card, which is the whole mechanic: five on offer, you pick one blind.
           A three-card fan was the first drawing and is the obvious one -- at 15px it collapses
           into a blob that reads as a crown. *@
        case "wonder":
            <g fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round">
                <rect x="6" y="3.5" width="12" height="17" rx="1.8" />
                <path d="M9.7 9.4a2.3 2.3 0 1 1 3.3 2.1c-.7.4-1 .9-1 1.6v.3" />
            </g>
            <circle cx="12" cy="16.7" r=".85" fill="currentColor" />
            break;

        @* Two arrows, one each way: a trade is a swap of equals. Offset vertically and with
           heads, which is what keeps it clear of the three even lines on the More button.
           A two-arrow cycle was the alternative and reads as refresh -- this app has sync. *@
        case "trades":
            <path d="M4 9.5h13M14 6.5l3 3-3 3M20 15.5H7M10 12.5l-3 3 3 3"
                  fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" />
            break;

        @* A clipboard: the list you retype the in-game board from, which is what that page is
           for. Drawing the twenty slots themselves was the first attempt and the honest one --
           at 15px twenty slots inside a frame mush into a filled grey rectangle, and four large
           ones instead collide with the collection's four panes. *@
        case "board":
            <g fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round">
                <rect x="4.5" y="4" width="15" height="17" rx="2" />
                <path d="M9 4.6V2.8h6v1.8" />
                <path d="M8.5 10.5h7M8.5 14.5h4.5" />
            </g>
            break;

        @* Two people, because what is being compared is two accounts. Two bars of different
           heights collided with the progress marks -- a count difference and nothing else --
           and two overlapping circles read as one blob at 15px rather than as an overlap.
           This is the only person in the set, so it cannot be mistaken for anything. *@
        case "compare":
            <g fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round">
                <circle cx="8.5" cy="8" r="2.8" />
                <path d="M3.5 19a5 5 0 0 1 10 0" />
                <circle cx="17" cy="9.5" r="2.3" />
                <path d="M13.6 19a4.2 4.2 0 0 1 7.9-1.6" />
            </g>
            break;

        @* An hourglass, which is the app's own word for the thing that page is about: three
           currencies that regenerate, and the hourglasses that bring them forward. *@
        case "resources":
            <path d="M7 3h10M7 21h10M8.5 3v3.4L12 10.8l3.5-4.4V3M8.5 21v-3.4L12 13.2l3.5 4.4V21"
                  fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" />
            break;

        case "history":
            <g fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round">
                <circle cx="12" cy="12" r="8.2" />
                <path d="M12 7.2V12l3.6 2.6" />
            </g>
            break;

        @* Sliders rather than a gear. A gear is a circle with radial strokes and so is the sun
           on the theme button two inches away -- at 15px they were the same drawing. The knobs
           are what keep this clear of the More button's three even lines, so they are filled in
           the surface colour rather than left open. *@
        case "settings":
            <g fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.7" stroke-linecap="round">
                <path d="M5 7h14M5 12h14M5 17h14" />
                <circle cx="9" cy="7" r="2.1" fill="var(--bs-body-bg)" />
                <circle cx="15" cy="12" r="2.1" fill="var(--bs-body-bg)" />
                <circle cx="8" cy="17" r="2.1" fill="var(--bs-body-bg)" />
            </g>
            break;
    }
</svg>

@code {
    /// <summary>
    /// Which destination's mark to draw: the route's own name -- "collection", "progress",
    /// "packs", "more", "chase", "decks", "wonder", "trades", "board", "compare", "resources",
    /// "history" or "settings".
    ///
    /// A string rather than an enum because the callers are markup and the value is written once
    /// per nav entry beside the href it belongs to -- an enum would put the two spellings of one
    /// destination on different lines. An unknown name draws nothing rather than throwing: a nav
    /// with a missing glyph is a nav, and a nav that threw would take the whole shell down.
    ///
    /// The booster pack is NOT here. It comes from <see cref="PackMark"/>, which the wordmark and
    /// the centre tab already share.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public string For { get; set; } = "";

    /// <summary>
    /// Rendered size in px. 22 beside a tab label, where the tab is the target; 15 beside a
    /// desktop nav button's word, where the word is.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public int Size { get; set; } = 22;

    /// <summary>Class on the svg, so a caller can size it from the stylesheet instead.</summary>
    [Parameter] public string? Class { get; set; }
}
